Description:
CRASH CAUSE. InProcessTickBus.Publish iterates all subscriber channels and blocks on `case ch <- tick` when any subscriber channel is full. Under simulationSpeedMultiplier>=8 the CandleGenerator subscriber falls behind (9-interval processing under engine lock) causing the channel to fill in seconds. Publisher blocks, goroutines stack, OOM crash.

FIX: Replace the blocking select with a non-blocking select + default drop:
  select {
  case ch <- tick:
  default:
      atomic.AddUint64(&b.droppedTicks, 1)
      // optional: log at WARN once per second via rate-limiter
  }

Also fix the RLock-while-blocking issue: copy the subscribers slice under RLock first, release the lock, THEN iterate and send. This prevents Subscribe() from deadlocking while Publish() is stalled.

Add `droppedTicks uint64` field to InProcessTickBus. Expose via a Stats() method. Hook into the existing monitoring log every 5s.

DOUBLE GOROUTINE BUG. Two goroutines drain the same `candleDBFlushCh`:
  1. app.go:135  – `candle_db_writer` service calls go app.CandleGenerator.StartCandleDBWriter(ctx)
  2. candles.go:217 – StartCandleGeneration() internally calls go cg.StartCandleDBWriter(ctx)

This causes non-deterministic batch splitting, incomplete OHLCV DB writes, and possible GORM CreateInBatches panic with empty batch.

FIX: Delete the entire `candle_db_writer` serviceAdapter block from app.go (lines 130-137). The internal call inside StartCandleGeneration is the single correct launch point.

Also delete the commented-out stop func for candle_generator (lines 142-144) and wire it properly:
  stop: func(ctx context.Context) error {
      return app.CandleGenerator.Stop(ctx)
  }

